t all that apply." :question/worked-solution "All components have continuous first partial derivatives on $\\mathbb R^2$. Polynomial, exponential, trigonometric and arctangent functions are smooth here; any logarithm has positive argument and any denominator is strictly positive. Thus $\\mathbf V=P\\,\\mathbf i+Q\\,\\mathbf j$ is **conservative** exactly when $P_y=Q_x$ everywhere.\n\nFor $\\mathbf F$, differentiate each component with respect to the indicated variable:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P&=y \\cos{\\left(2 x y \\right)} + 2 y,\\\\Q&=x \\cos{\\left(2 x y \\right)} + 2 x + 1,\\\\P_y&=- 2 x y \\sin{\\left(2 x y \\right)} + \\cos{\\left(2 x y \\right)} + 2,\\\\Q_x&=- 2 x y \\sin{\\left(2 x y \\right)} + \\cos{\\left(2 x y \\right)} + 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$P_y=Q_x$ is an identity, so $\\mathbf F$ is conservative.\n\nFor $\\mathbf G$, differentiate each component with respect to the indicated variable:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P&=- 2 x \\sin{\\left(y^{2} \\right)} - 2 y,\\\\Q&=- 2 x - 1,\\\\P_y&=- 4 x y \\cos{\\left(y^{2} \\right)} - 2,\\\\Q_x&=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivatives are not equal everywhere, so $\\mathbf G$ is not conservative. At $(1,1)$, the difference is $- 4 \\cos{\\left(1 \\right)}\\ne0$.\n\nFor $\\mathbf H$, differentiate each component with respect to the indicated variable:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P&=- 4 x \\sin{\\left(y^{2} \\right)} - y - 1,\\\\Q&=- 4 x^{2} y \\cos{\\left(y^{2} \\right)} - x,\\\\P_y&=- 8 x y \\cos{\\left(y^{2} \\right)} - 1,\\\\Q_x&=- 8 x y \\cos{\\left(y^{2} \\right)} - 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$P_y=Q_x$ is an identity, so $\\mathbf H$ is conservative.\n\nSelect $\\mathbf F$, $\\mathbf H$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718690 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9584b9e9-ff18-5944-ae73-14484c543299" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186718687} {:answer/id #uuid "cb2de09d-81c7-56e0-b856-004843ea3263" :answer/typ